{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' sensitivity-table.md && printf '\\\\n--- memo ---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' memo.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Reference-category sensitivity and marginal means\n\nAll entries are IRR-corrected profile-level estimates from `projoint`; 95% confidence intervals are in brackets. The repeated task estimates tau = 0.172. Marginal means are baseline-invariant.\n\n## Violent Crime Rate AMCEs under each baseline\n\nEach non-reference cell is the AMCE for the row level relative to the column's reference level.\n\n| Level | 20% Less Crime Than National Average | 20% More Crime Than National Average |\n|---|---|---|\n| 20% Less Crime Than National Average | 0.000 (reference) | 0.251 [0.201, 0.301] |\n| 20% More Crime Than National Average | -0.251 [-0.301, -0.201] | 0.000 (reference) |\n\nThe crime attribute is binary, so changing the reference merely reverses the sign of the one pairwise contrast; its absolute magnitude is unchanged.\n\n## Multi-level check: Housing Cost AMCEs under each baseline\n\nWith three levels, the numerical AMCE attached to a level changes with the selected reference, although every pairwise contrast is the same comparison regardless of coding.\n\n| Level | 15% of pre-tax income | 30% of pre-tax income | 40% of pre-tax income |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 15% of pre-tax income | 0.000 (reference) | 0.137 [0.080, 0.194] | 0.198 [0.140, 0.256] |\n| 30% of pre-tax income | -0.137 [-0.194, -0.080] | 0.000 (reference) | 0.061 [0.005, 0.117] |\n| 40% of pre-tax income | -0.198 [-0.256, -0.140] | -0.061 [-0.117, -0.005] | 0.000 (reference) |\n\n## Marginal means for all levels\n\n| Attribute | Level | Marginal mean [95% CI] |\n|---|---|---|\n| Housing Cost | 15% of pre-tax income | 0.614 [0.571, 0.657] |\n| Housing Cost | 30% of pre-tax income | 0.477 [0.436, 0.517] |\n| Housing Cost | 40% of pre-tax income | 0.416 [0.372, 0.459] |\n| Presidential Vote (2020) | 30% Democrat, 70% Republican | 0.483 [0.438, 0.528] |\n| Presidential Vote (2020) | 50% Democrat, 50% Republican | 0.536 [0.496, 0.576] |\n| Presidential Vote (2020) | 70% Democrat, 30% Republican | 0.480 [0.436, 0.524] |\n| Racial Composition | 50% White, 50% Nonwhite | 0.500 [0.456, 0.544] |\n| Racial Composition | 75% White, 25% Nonwhite | 0.537 [0.492, 0.581] |\n| Racial Composition | 90% White, 10% Nonwhite | 0.477 [0.437, 0.518] |\n| Racial Composition | 96% White, 4% Nonwhite | 0.488 [0.442, 0.534] |\n| School Quality | 5 out of 10 | 0.442 [0.401, 0.484] |\n| School Quality | 9 out of 10 | 0.558 [0.516, 0.599] |\n| Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Errands | 10 min | 0.610 [0.567, 0.654] |\n| Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Errands | 25 min | 0.541 [0.498, 0.584] |\n| Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Errands | 45 min | 0.470 [0.425, 0.514] |\n| Total Daily Driving Time for Commuting and Errands | 75 min | 0.373 [0.329, 0.417] |\n| Type of Place | City – downtown, with a mix of offices, apartments, and shops | 0.394 [0.342, 0.446] |\n| Type of Place | City, more residential area | 0.509 [0.454, 0.564] |\n| Type of Place | Rural area | 0.529 [0.474, 0.583] |\n| Type of Place | Small town | 0.552 [0.499, 0.605] |\n| Type of Place | Suburban neighborhood with houses only | 0.473 [0.420, 0.526] |\n| Type of Place | Suburban neighborhood with mix of shops, houses, businesses | 0.540 [0.489, 0.591] |\n| Violent Crime Rate (Vs National Rate) | 20% Less Crime Than National Average | 0.626 [0.584, 0.667] |\n| Violent Crime Rate (Vs National Rate) | 20% More Crime Than National Average | 0.374 [0.333, 0.416] |\n\nFigure caption: Endpoint marginal means (with 95% confidence intervals) for each attribute. Lines span the minimum and maximum observed marginal mean within each attribute; blue triangles identify the two crime-rate levels. The figure uses 400 respondents and 8 paired tasks per respondent (plus one flipped repeat); MMs are IRR-corrected profile-selection probabilities.\n\n--- memo ---\n# Reply to the reviewer\n\nThank you for identifying an important presentational issue. The reviewer is mechanically correct: an AMCE is a contrast, so its sign and its numerical value are defined relative to a named reference level. A table that lists coefficients without making those contrasts explicit can invite an unwarranted reading of a reference category as substantively privileged. We will revise the presentation accordingly.\n\nThe sensitivity analysis nonetheless does not support the stronger conclusion that the crime result is an artifact of coding. Violent Crime Rate has only two experimentally presented levels: 20% less versus 20% more crime than the national average. With low crime as the reference, the AMCE for high crime is -0.251 (95% CI [-0.301, -0.201]); with high crime as the reference, the AMCE for low crime is +0.251 ([0.201, 0.301]). Rebaselining reverses the sign, as it must, but not the magnitude, uncertainty interval, or substantive comparison.\n\nThe concern has more force for multi-level attributes. For Housing Cost, the AMCE for 40% rather than 15% of pre-tax income is -0.198; relative to the 30% level, it is -0.061; and the contrast for 15% rather than 40% is +0.198. These are different named pairwise comparisons, not competing estimates of a single level's intrinsic effect. We will therefore report AMCEs as explicitly labeled contrasts rather than treat a coefficient's magnitude as an attribute-wide importance score.\n\nOur principal evidence will be the baseline-invariant marginal means (MMs), reported for every level. The MM is 0.626 (95% CI [0.584, 0.667]) for 20% less crime and 0.374 ([0.333, 0.416]) for 20% more crime. Thus the displayed crime levels span 0.251 in profile-selection probability. This is the largest observed endpoint span among the attributes in this design, but it is only slightly larger than the 0.237 span for daily driving time. The figure and table make that comparison transparent.\n\nWe will consequently replace the headline claim that crime \"drives\" community choice. The revised manuscript will state: *within the attribute levels presented in this conjoint, communities with 20% less rather than 20% more violent crime were substantially more likely to be selected, and this contrast was the largest observed MM endpoint difference, narrowly exceeding commuting time.* We will not claim that crime uniquely dominates other considerations, that the rank is statistically established, or that this design-specific range generalizes beyond the levels studied.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
